      <h1>Computing Without a Center: From Pure Optimality to Proof of Useful Work &amp; the Univalent Semantic Web</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        For fifty years, computer science, cryptography, finance, and the open web pursued five seemingly separate dreams: a web of machine-readable meaning, a permanent decentralized internet, trustless money and markets, self-hosting metacircular languages, and compounding collective intelligence. Every single one stalled at the exact same bottleneck—assuming that coordination requires a <strong>fixed point of control or global agreement</strong>. Starting from zero prerequisites, this guide derives the entire mathematical and computational stack forced by maximizing rational capacity, information accessibility, local agency, and manipulability—culminating in <strong>Proof of Useful Work (PoUW)</strong> and <strong>programs operating on programs in constant, paradox-free flux</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="meta-line">
        Every theorem, invariant, and benchmark below corresponds directly to live code in <code>store/</code> (<code>lib/credit.hypercubical</code>, <code>lib/dht.hypercubical</code>, <code>lib/distnet.hypercubical</code>, <code>lib/portal.hypercubical</code>, <code>lib/singularity.hypercubical</code>, <code>lib/hx-node.hypercubical</code>, <code>experiments/cxx/hx-book.hypercubical</code>).
      </div>
