    <section class="chapter" id="ch1">
      <div class="ch-num">Chapter 01 · The Diagnosis</div>
      <h2>Five Dreams of Technology—and the One Hidden Axiom That Broke Them All</h2>
      <p>
        If you care about crypto, decentralized finance, peer-to-peer networks, open-source software, AI, or the original dream of the World Wide Web, you have already watched the same tragic cycle repeat five times:
      </p>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>1. The Semantic Web (Berners-Lee)</h4>
          <p><strong>The Dream:</strong> A web where machines share structured meaning and combine discoveries automatically across every site.<br><strong>Where It Broke:</strong> URLs point to mutable servers (link rot), and RDF/OWL/JSON-LD are unverified claims with no executable proofs. Two schemas expressing the same concept cannot automatically translate results.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>2. Content-Addressed Web (Git / IPFS)</h4>
          <p><strong>The Dream:</strong> Name data by its cryptographic hash so no server owns it and nobody can silently tamper with it.<br><strong>Where It Broke:</strong> Stopped at opaque byte blobs. A hash tells you the bytes didn't change, not what the bytes <em>mean</em>, whether the code inside them is safe, or how to compose them with another project.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>3. Crypto &amp; DeFi (Bitcoin / Ethereum)</h4>
          <p><strong>The Dream:</strong> Trustless, permissionless global value transfer and financial markets without banks or clearinghouses.<br><strong>Where It Broke:</strong> Assumed safety requires a single global totally-ordered timeline (a blockchain). Proof of Work burns gigawatts on meaningless hashes; Proof of Stake detaches money from productive work; smart contracts remain untyped bug mines.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>4. Metacircular Computing (Lisp / Smalltalk)</h4>
          <p><strong>The Dream:</strong> Programs operating on programs operating on programs—total fluidity where users reshape syntax, compilers, and tools at will.<br><strong>Where It Broke:</strong> Without a typed kernel and guarded self-reference, macros become unreadable fragility, and compilers stay frozen as trusted black boxes (GCC/LLVM).</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        All four—plus the fifth dream of <strong>collective human+AI superintelligence</strong>—collided with one unconscious habit inherited from industrial-age institutions: <strong>the belief that order requires a fixed center</strong>. A central server. A canonical database. A single global blockchain head. A privileged compiler. A frozen syntax. A trusted referee.
      </p>
      <p>
        Look closely at physical reality (Rovelli's relational physics, finite speed of light): the universe itself has <strong>no global clock, no central ledger, and no privileged observer</strong>. Yet physical conservation laws hold everywhere with absolute precision through strictly local interactions. Computing works the exact same way the moment you strip away the center.
      </p>

