    <section class="chapter" id="ch2">
      <div class="ch-num">Chapter 02 · First-Principles Derivation</div>
      <h2>Deriving Math &amp; Computing from Scratch: What Optimality Forces</h2>
      <p>
        Forget every textbook convention you have ever seen. Suppose you start from an empty desk with only one objective: <strong>maximize rational capacity, information accessibility, and manipulability</strong> for every agent in a physical universe where signals travel at finite speed and memory is local. What architecture is <em>mathematically forced</em>?
      </p>

      <h3>Step 1 · Information Is Structure, Not Raw Bits</h3>
      <p>
        If a stream of symbols has predictable causal structure—meaning the past context $(x_{t-4}, x_{t-3}, x_{t-2}, x_{t-1})$ narrows down the next symbol $x_t$ to probability $p(x_t \mid \text{ctx})$—then Shannon proved its true information content is $-\log_2 p(x_t \mid \text{ctx})$ bits. Storing or transmitting repeated structure wastes rational capacity. Hyperfactoring asks the same question for <em>programs and proofs</em>: if five systems repeat the same algebraic law in five different languages, factor the law once and let every instance inherit it.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 2 · Naming by Content, Never by Location</h3>
      <p>
        Suppose you want maximum <strong>information accessibility and manipulability</strong>. If you reference an idea by <em>location</em> (<code>https://company.com/api/v2/file</code> or memory pointer <code>0x7ff8a10</code>), then moving the machine, changing owners, or editing the file silently breaks every dependent computation on Earth.
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead, name every mathematical object, type, proof, function, and document by the cryptographic hash of its normalized structure:
      </p>
      <div class="eq-box">
        $$\mathrm{key}(x) = \mathrm{SHA256}(\mathrm{canonical\_form}(x))$$
      </div>
      <p>
        Instantly, three properties become automatic: (1) any peer holding the hash can verify integrity locally without trusting who sent it; (2) immutable dependencies can never rot or suffer supply-chain hijacking; (3) identical discoveries made independently on opposite sides of the planet collide onto the exact same key—deduplicating human and AI knowledge by physics rather than committees.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 3 · Computation Is Local Graph Interaction, Not a Global Tape</h3>
      <p>
        Why did classical computing invent Turing's sequential tape and von Neumann's single CPU clock? Because 1940s vacuum tubes had to share one centralized control unit. That forces every parallel problem to pretend it is a single line of instructions—requiring locks, mutexes, cache coherence storms, and global schedulers just to let two cores work together.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ask what the minimal <em>local</em> model of computation is. In Yves Lafont's <strong>Interaction Nets</strong> (the execution core of Hyperactivity, <code>lib/distnet.hypercubical</code>), a program is simply a graph of agents connected by wires. Every agent has one <strong>principal port</strong>. Whenever two agents meet face-to-face on their principal ports (an <em>active pair</em>), they fire one of three purely local rules:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Annihilation (same tag):</strong> Two matching nodes cancel and wire their auxiliary ports straight together.</li>
        <li><strong>Commutation (different tags):</strong> Two distinct nodes pass cleanly through each other, cloning lazily only the exact subgraph demanded.</li>
        <li><strong>Erasure:</strong> An eraser node absorbs its partner and propagates locally—zero global garbage collector needed!</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="thm-box">
        <div class="thm-head"><span>Strong Confluence Without a Global Clock</span><span><code>lib/distnet.hypercubical</code> · <code>disjoint-cuts-commute</code></span></div>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0">
          Because every rewrite touches only the two endpoints of a single wire, any two disjoint active pairs $(a \bowtie b)$ on Peer A and $(c \bowtie d)$ on Peer B <strong>commute definitionally</strong>:
          $$\mathrm{step}_A(\mathrm{step}_B(G)) \;\equiv\; \mathrm{step}_B(\mathrm{step}_A(G))$$
          Parallelism is no longer an error-prone hack bolted on with threads and locks—it is a theorem of locality. Order of reduction never changes the answer.
        </p>
      </div>

