      <div class="ch-num">Chapter 03 · Types &amp; Univalent Portals</div>
      <h2>Types as Executable Contracts &amp; Equivalence as a Portal: The Real Semantic Web</h2>
      <p>
        Now we derive mathematics itself—without assuming any background in logic.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you write a program in JavaScript or C, a type like <code>int</code> tells you almost nothing: it cannot distinguish a negative balance from a valid quantity, a sorted list from an unsorted list, or a protein with an atomic clash from a physical minimum. So engineers write thousands of fragile unit tests that check five examples and pray the sixth doesn't explode in production.
      </p>
      <p>
        In <strong>dependent type theory</strong>, a type can express <em>any</em> mathematical statement—and a program of that type <em>is</em> its constructive proof (the Curry–Howard correspondence):
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Specification = Type:</strong> <code>(s : Scaffold) → leb (pocket-energy lead) (pocket-energy s) ≡ true</code> states: <em>"for every single one of all 64 receptor scaffolds, our lead molecule has energy less than or equal to it."</em></li>
        <li><strong>Proof = Program:</strong> Constructing an inhabitant of that type proves the claim for <em>all</em> cases simultaneously, verified deterministically by a tiny kernel.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Now add the breakthrough that finally fulfills Tim Berners-Lee's Semantic Web dream: <strong>Cubical Univalence</strong> (<code>lib/portal.hypercubical</code>).
      </p>
      <p>
        In ordinary software—and in the old RDF/JSON-LD Semantic Web—if Team A encodes data as a linked list and Team B encodes the exact same mathematical structure as a binary tree or a packed byte array, every library, theorem, and optimizer written by Team A is useless to Team B until someone manually rewrites it.
      </p>
      <p>
        In cubical type theory, an isomorphism (a round-trip equivalence) between two representations $A$ and $B$ produces a first-class <strong>path</strong> $e : A \equiv B$. Given <em>any</em> property or theorem $P(B)$ proved anywhere on the network, <strong>cubical transport</strong> delivers $P(A)$ directly into your workspace with zero manual porting:
      </p>
      <div class="eq-box">
        $$\mathrm{portal\_arrive} \;:\; (P : \mathrm{Type} \to \mathrm{Type}) \to (e : A \equiv B) \to P(B) \to P(A)$$
        $$\mathrm{portal\_arrive\_chain} \;:\; \mathrm{IdChain}_n(A, B) \to P(B) \to P(A)$$
      </div>
      <p>
        Think about what this means for the Semantic Web: **meaning is no longer dead text labels glued to URLs.** Meaning is a live network of content-addressed types connected by computational equivalences. Prove a theorem once in <em>any</em> representation, and it arrives instantaneously across every isomorphic domain on Earth.
      </p>

