      <h2>Conserved Distributed Credit With No Global Scheduler</h2>
      <p>
        For readers coming from crypto and finance, here is the moment everything shifts.
      </p>
      <p>
        Why did Satoshi Nakamoto invent a global totally-ordered chain of blocks? Because if everyone shares one global ledger table, you need a global clock to decide whether Alice paid Bob before Alice paid Carol. That single design choice created every scaling limit in crypto: block times, mempool MEV, gas auctions, state bloat, and global validator bottlenecks.
      </p>
      <p>
        In <code>docs/hyperactivity.hypercubical</code> (v32, Part I §10.3 <em>"A distributed credit invariant, with no global scheduler as an axiom"</em>) and <code>lib/credit.hypercubical</code>, we model distributed value the way physics models conservation of energy and charge—<strong>strictly locally</strong>:
      </p>
      <div class="eq-box">
        $$S = (c,\; m), \qquad \mathrm{total}(S) \;=\; \sum_{i} c_i \;+\; \sum_{i,j} m_{ij} \;=\; \text{constant}$$
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>$c_i$</strong> is the credit currently held by peer $i$.</li>
        <li><strong>$m_{ij}$</strong> is the credit currently in flight on the directed channel from peer $i$ to peer $j$.</li>
        <li><strong>Local Allocation (Eq. 10.7):</strong> Peer $i$ can only allocate $q \le c_i$ into channel $m_{ij}$ with a fresh receipt identifier $r$. Locally, $c_i' = c_i - q$ and $m_{ij}' = m_{ij} + q$. Total credit is unchanged.</li>
        <li><strong>Receipt-Bearing Delivery (Eq. 10.8):</strong> Peer $j$ verifies that receipt $r$ has never been delivered before, moves $q$ from $m_{ij}$ into $c_j$, and marks $r$ spent. Any replayed or duplicated message carrying $r$ is rejected deterministically.</li>
        <li><strong>Observer Fibers (Eq. 10.9):</strong> An observer never needs a global snapshot of the planet's balances. Their knowledge is the exact <em>fiber</em> of global states compatible with their own local allocations and receipts—and conservation holds identically across every compatible history, regardless of network interleaving!</li>
      </ul>

